  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n    Central idea: We will use a **variable-resolution optimization** combined with **momentum-based gradient descent** and **binary-coordinate descent**, but with adaptive resolution and feature-aware momentum. Unlike the parent, we will:\n    - Start with a coarse grid (n=100) and gradually increase resolution while storing and building upon prior solutions\n    - Apply feature-aware momentum that gives extra boosting to positions where significant improvements are detected in lag correlations\n    - Introduce a \"binary polish\" phase that forces the solution to extreme values near 0 or 1 in lag-sensitive regions to better approximate true thresholds\n\n    Why it improves: \n    - The parent's algorithm gets stuck in local minima due to uniform momentum application. Our technique adaptively amplifies progress in directions that reduce correlation at the most problematic lags\n    - Our binary-based refine step captures the discontinuities in the ideal h(x) that emerge at optimal resolutions, moving beyond smooth approximations\n    - The multi-resolution transfer escapes local extrema found at lower dimensions by exploring finer details\n\n    Limitations: \n    - Only proportional improvement to current best (\u22480.3808) is possible within budget\n    - Requires sophisticated gradient tracking and adaptive filtering\n    - Lacks theoretical guarantees on global optimality but demonstrates strong empirical performance on the discrete problem\n\n    Constraints implementation: We will ensure all candidate h satisfies [0,1] bounds and sum(n/2) at every reconstruction via:\n\n    ```python\n    def ensure_feasibility(h, n):\n        s = np.sum(h)\n        if not abs(s - n/2) < 1e-4:\n            h = h * (n/2) / s\n            h = np.clip(h, 0, 1)\n            diff = n/2 - np.sum(h)\n            while abs(diff) > 1e-4:\n                min_idx = np.argmin(h)\n                h[min_idx] += 0.1\n                max_idx = np.argmax(h)\n                h[max_idx] -= 0.1\n                diff = n/2 - np.sum(h)\n                ifh[i] < 0 or h[i] > 1:\n                    h[i] = np.clip(h[i], 0, 1)\n        return h\n    ```\n\n 2. Mathematical specification\n    - Variables: h \u2208 \u211d\u207f, 0 \u2264 h i \u2264 1 \u2200i\n    - Objective: minimize max_k \u2211_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i](1-h[i+k]) * dx, with dx=2/n and k\u2208[-(n-1),n-1]\n    - Constraints: \n        \u2211_{i} h_i = n/2 (exactly)\n        0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 (element-wise)\n\n    - Lag representation: We treat shifts as discrete and consider both positive and negative lags\n    - Feature-aware norm: We weight gradients by their statistical impact on the correlated regions (using lag correlation variance)\n\n 3. Implementation procedure\n    Phase 1: Coarse-to-fine resolution with momentum\n    ```python\n    # Initialization\n    try:\n        n_best = len(initial_h_values)\n        h_best = initial_h_values.copy()\n    except:\n        n_start = 100\n        h_best = np.random.rand(n_start)\n    n_init = min(100, n_best) if n_best > 100 else n_start\n\n    # Variable step momentum (v)\n    def momentum_phase(h, n, max_iter=1000):\n        \u03c4 = [1.2] * n  # Per-feature momentum decay\n        best_c = compute_c5(h, n)\n        grad = grad_smooth_max(h, n, alpha=20)  # Weighted max gradient\n        v = np.zeros(n)\n        vw = 0.8 * (grad < 0)  # Initial velocity toward improving features\n\n        for iter in range(max_iter):\n            if time > deadline - 50:\n                break\n            g = grad_smooth_max(h, n, alpha=20)\n            # Feature-based decoupled momentum\n            for i in range(n):\n                s = abs(g[i])\n                if s > 1e-5:\n                    vw[i: i+int(2*s)] += s / np.sum(g) * \u03c4[i]  # Statistical boosting for impactful features\n\n            h_new = h + vw\n            h_new = ensure_feasibility(h_new, n)\n            c_new = compute_c5(h_new, n)\n\n            if c_new < best_c - 1e-5:\n                best_c = c_new\n                vw = vw * 1.05  # Amplify successful momentum patterns\n                h = h_new.copy()\n            else:\n                vw = vw * 0.98  # Dampen unproductive direction\n\n            # Add lag-frequency regularization\n            lags = compute_all_lags(h, n)\n            vw = vw + 0.01 * (np.max(lags) - np.min(lags))\n\n        return h, best_c\n    ```\n\n    Phase 2: Feature-focused binary polish\n    ```python\n    def binary_polish(h, n, deadline):\n        top_lags = np.argsort(compute_all_lags(h, n))[-10:]  # Lag indices contributing most to correlation\n        best_h = h.copy()\n        patience = 0\n        best_c = compute_c5(h, n)\n\n        while time < deadline - 100:\n            for lag_idx in top_lags:\n                lag = lag_idx - (n-1) if lag_idx <= n-1 else lag_idx - 2*n+2\n                if abs(lag) > n/2: continue\n                affected_idx = abs(lag)\n\n                # Create binary perturbation in Lag-sensitive regions\n                improvement = False\n                for shift in [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]:\n                    h.copy() -> only perturb regions where h[i] is significant or near zero\n                    i1, i2 = affected_idx, affected_idx + shift if lag>0 else affected_idx-shift\n                    if 0 <= i1 <= n-1 and 0 <= i2 <= n-1:\n                        h[i1] = min(1, max(0, h[i1] + shift*I(roll_given_benefit)))\n                        h -= ensure_feasibility(h, n)\n\n                        c_old = best_c\n                        c_new = compute_c5(h, n)\n                        if c_new < c_old:\n                            best_c = c_new\n                            best_h = h.copy()\n                            improvement = True\n\n                if not improvement:\n                    patience += 1\n                    if patience > 10: break\n\n            return best_h, best_c\n    ```\n\n    End-to-end execution:\n    ```python\n    deadline = current_start + budget_s\n    h_curr = h_best.copy()\n    n_curr = min(500, n_best)\n\n    # Multi-resolution pass (10-12 steps)\n    n_candidates = [200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700]\n    for n_target in n_candidates:\n        if time > deadline - 200:\n            break\n        if n_target > n_curr:\n            # Interpolate fine-to-coarse instead of uniform, reducing information loss\n            x_orig = np.linspace(0, 2, n_curr)\n            x_new = np.linspace(0, 2, n_target)\n            h_new = np.interp(x_new, x_orig, h_curr)\n\n        h_curr, best_c = momentum_phase(h_new, n_target, deadline)\n        # Save intermediate best solution\n\n    # Final polishing\n    h_curr, unused = binary_polish(h_curr, n_target, deadline)\n    h_curr = ensure_feasibility(h_curr, n_target)\n    best_c = compute_c5(h_curr, n_target)\n    return h_curr, best_c, n_target\n    ```\n\n 4. Search choices and variations\n    - Base resolution: 200 (instead of parent's 500) balances quality and cost\n    - Lag selection: Always prioritize lags that contribute to maximum correlation in the smoothed objective\n    - Momentum tuner: Decay rate \u03c4[i] adjusts dynamically based on improvement rate: \u03c4[i] = 1.05 if improvement 0.6 recent 1-2 steps, 0.95 if no improvement\n    - Stochastic feature selection: In perturbation steps, only update 5-25% of features in bounce passes for dimensionality\n    - Start-lag enhancement: Top 5 lags (\u2208 [-8,8] range) receive double penalty-reduction during restarts\n\n 5. Compute budget and robustness\n    - Dominant cost: The momentum phase (gradient computation + vector operations) dominates; expect to run 20-30 iterations at n=700 within 1000s\n    - Memory: Stores all saved solutions from every resolution (max storing ~6 representations for 7 steps)\n    - Graceful degrading: \n        If time remaining < 60s, skip binary polish and use lag-frequency pruning\n        If correlation calculation fails, reduce lag samples by half + recurse\n    - Fall back: Project any candidate solution to satisfy constraint via: \n        `def repair(h): ... use midpoint interpolation to normalize sum(h)`\n\n 6. Validation and return contract\n    - Final candidate check:\n        h_final = ensure_feasibility(candidate)\n        assert np.all(0 <= h_final <= 1)\n        assert abs(sum(h_final) - n/2) < 1e-5\n        c5 = compute_c5(h_final, n)\n        run additional validate_binary = np.isclose(c5, 1), but function compute_c5 is authoritative\n\n    - Sanitty checks:\n        must be np.isfinite(c5)\n        np.max(compute_all_lags(h, n)) * dx must be similar to c5\n\n    - Output format: (h_values, C_5_value, n_points)\n\n    - Escalators:\n        If dx calculation fails, use dx=2/float(n)\n        If compute_all_lags takes too long, reduce max_lag to 40\n        If h_out-of-bound: set np.clip(h, 0, 1) and re-normalize",
